{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789224443,"nonce":"UyVItEbDjaBMeecnlJqM5a7kuyI=","title":"M:N fiber scheduling across all cores","message":"Fibers are N:1 today: a krio stackful coroutine pinned to the thread\nthat created it. The host handle (zyntax_embed::fiber::ZyntaxFiber) is\n!Send and every resume, abort and drop checks the creator thread. The\nscheduler is cooperative on the driving thread; parallelism comes from\nthe band pool, which runs closures on worker threads, not fibers.\n\nTarget: Go-shaped M:N with cooperative yields. N worker threads (one per\ncore, the band pool's workers) each own a run queue, steal from each\nother, and a fiber may resume on any of them. Context switches are\nalready thread-agnostic and each fiber already carries its own\nhandler-stack segment layered on a baseline, so the switching primitive\nand the effect state are not the obstacle.\n\nWhat has to be true first, in order:\n\n1. Closure environments on the heap (b1e62721f1e6c8eca4f5e21b8914d0ff0d5e2f629f8697b140c59a46bedb962b).\n   A fiber whose captures point into the spawning frame cannot outlive\n   or leave that frame's thread. Prerequisite, not optional.\n\n2. A Send discipline at spawn.\n   Work stealing is sound only if what crosses threads may cross. The\n   ownership model is the tool: own captures may cross, borrows may\n   not, and the borrow checker enforces it at the spawn site. That\n   depends on borrow_check running on the tiered profiles (7f6ba11ba98503c48cc76bfe1d44202382b248548a7277ffb1c55c9961db0fbe).\n\n3. Runtime state a fiber touches on resume is per-fiber or reloaded.\n   The handler-stack baseline (today the spawning thread's), the pool\n   allocator's free path (freed on worker B what worker A allocated),\n   and anything cached from a thread-local in a fiber's stack. Each is\n   audited and either travels with the fiber or is re-read after every\n   resume.\n\n4. Preemption is a second step.\n   Cooperative only at first, like Tokio: a fiber that never yields\n   pins a worker. The OSR back-edge probes on tier-0 code are the place\n   to check a preemption flag, the same spot Go instruments, if and when\n   it is needed.\n\n5. Stacks stay fixed.\n   Native code with raw pointers cannot relocate a stack, so fibers\n   keep fixed mmap'd stacks and the fiber count is bounded by address\n   space and commit policy, lower than Go's.\n\nOut of scope: a netpoller. Host futures already integrate through the\nasync executor; the scheduler needs a wake path from them, not an event\nloop of its own.\n\nThe host-facing ZyntaxFiber stays thread-affine; M:N applies to fibers\nspawned and driven inside the runtime.","files":null}]}